--- DESCRIPTION ---
How the Paramount decree, widescreen formats, Steadicam and early CGI reshaped cinema.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Paramount Decree and the End of the Studio System: 1948</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">In 1948, the United States Supreme Court ruled in <em>United States v. Paramount Pictures, Inc.</em> that the major Hollywood studios had illegally monopolised the film industry. The studios had operated a vertically integrated system: they produced films, distributed them and owned the theatres where audiences watched them. The court decision &mdash; known as the <dfn class="term" data-def="The 1948 US Supreme Court ruling that forced the major Hollywood studios to sell off their cinema chains, ending vertical integration of production, distribution and exhibition.">Paramount decree</dfn> &mdash; forced the studios to divest their cinema chains. Without guaranteed screen access, studios could no longer be certain of a return on every film they made. The comfortable industrial certainties of the studio system began to erode.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The Paramount decree coincided with a second existential threat: the arrival of television in American homes. By the early 1950s, millions of households had a small screen in the living room that provided free entertainment. Cinema attendances fell sharply. The studios responded with a technological strategy: give audiences something their television set could never offer. The answer was spectacle &mdash; bigger, wider, louder, more immersive.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what did the Paramount decree (1948) force studios to do, and which second threat was cinema facing at the same time?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The 1948 Paramount decree forced Hollywood studios to sell off their cinema chains, ending vertical integration. Combined with the rise of television, it pushed studios towards spectacular widescreen technologies to draw audiences back to cinemas.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Widescreen Formats: CinemaScope and the 1950s Revolution</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The most visible technological response to television was the widescreen format. Standard cinema of the 1930s and 1940s used an aspect ratio of approximately 1.37:1 &mdash; a near-square frame not much wider than a television screen. In 1953, 20th Century Fox introduced <dfn class="term" data-def="A widescreen film process introduced in 1953 that used an anamorphic lens to squeeze a wide image onto standard 35mm film, producing an aspect ratio of approximately 2.55:1.">CinemaScope</dfn>, using an anamorphic lens system to squeeze a panoramic image onto standard 35mm film. The resulting ratio &mdash; approximately 2.55:1 &mdash; was nearly twice as wide as the television image and completely transformed how directors composed their shots.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Paramount&rsquo;s rival process, VistaVision, used a different approach: running the film horizontally through the camera rather than vertically, doubling the area of each frame and producing exceptional sharpness. Other studios experimented with three-projector systems (Cinerama) and even 3D processes that required audiences to wear polarised glasses. Most of these formats proved too complex or expensive to sustain; CinemaScope and similar anamorphic processes dominated the widescreen era and still inform the standard theatrical aspect ratios used today.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why widescreen changed how directors think</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">A wide frame is not simply a bigger frame &mdash; it is a different compositional challenge. In a near-square frame, the director controls where the eye goes by placing the subject centrally; the sides of the frame are relatively empty. In a widescreen frame, the sides become active: two characters can be held in genuine spatial tension at opposite edges, or the environment can dwarf the human figure in ways that carry thematic weight. Early critics complained that CinemaScope was only suited to &ldquo;snakes and funerals&rdquo; &mdash; things that are naturally horizontal. Over time, directors learned to activate the full width of the frame for intimate drama as well as epic spectacle. This shift in compositional thinking is directly relevant to your analysis of <dfn class="term" data-def="The arrangement of visual elements within the camera&rsquo;s frame, including the placement of subjects, use of depth and relationship to the edges of the image.">framing</dfn> in any film made after the mid-1950s.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Lightweight Cameras and the French New Wave</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The 1950s and 1960s also saw the development of smaller, lighter cameras that could be carried on the shoulder and operated without a tripod or dolly. <dfn class="term" data-def="A documentary-influenced style of filmmaking using lightweight, handheld cameras to achieve a rough, spontaneous visual quality, associated with movements including the French New Wave and cin&eacute;ma v&eacute;rit&eacute;.">Handheld cinematography</dfn> had been pioneered in documentary work, where the ability to follow events without setting up heavy equipment was essential. In the late 1950s, a group of young French directors &mdash; including Jean-Luc Godard and Fran&ccedil;ois Truffaut &mdash; adopted documentary aesthetics deliberately in fiction films, rejecting the polished studio look in favour of location shooting, natural light and a camera that moved impulsively with the action. The resulting films felt urgent, alive and deliberately rough-edged in a way that studio filmmaking could not replicate.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The French New Wave influence spread across cinema worldwide in the 1960s and 1970s. The shaky, spontaneous handheld image became associated with psychological realism and documentary authenticity, a connotation it retains today. The camera no longer needed to be a fixed, authoritative observer; it could be implicated in the action, uncertain, reactive &mdash; a participant rather than a recorder.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: what was the Steadicam, who invented it, and in which year was it first used on a major production?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The Steadicam was invented by Garrett Brown and first used on a major production in 1975&ndash;1976 (notably on <em>Rocky</em> and <em>Bound for Glory</em> in 1976). It allowed cameras to move fluidly through space without the jerkiness of handheld work or the constraint of tracks and dollies.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">The Steadicam: Garrett Brown and the Floating Camera</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">In 1975, American inventor and cameraman Garrett Brown developed the <dfn class="term" data-def="A camera-stabilisation harness worn by the camera operator that isolates the camera from the operator&rsquo;s body movements, allowing smooth, fluid tracking shots without the need for rails or a dolly.">Steadicam</dfn>, a body-mounted stabilisation system that used a counterbalanced arm to isolate the camera from the operator&rsquo;s movements. The Steadicam could follow an actor through a door, up a staircase, around a corner, into a crowd &mdash; all in one continuous shot &mdash; while the image remained perfectly smooth. It combined the freedom of handheld work with the visual composure of a dolly shot.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The Steadicam&rsquo;s effect on filmmaking was immediate. Stanley Kubrick used it extensively in <em>The Shining</em> (1980) to track a child on a tricycle along hotel corridors: the camera glided behind him at precisely his height, following with an uncanny, unhurried smoothness that felt neither like a tracking dolly (which would be mechanical and distant) nor a handheld camera (which would feel urgent and human). The sensation was of something inhuman, patient and predatory &mdash; a feeling produced entirely by the specific technology of the Steadicam applied with deliberate intent. This is the kind of link between micro-choice (camera stabilisation system) and macro-meaning (psychological dread, threat) that strong film analysis requires.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Early CGI: Industrial Light and Magic, Terminator 2 and Jurassic Park</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">George Lucas founded Industrial Light &amp; Magic (ILM) in 1975 to produce the effects for <em>Star Wars</em> (1977). Whilst <em>Star Wars</em> relied primarily on physical models, optical printing and practical effects rather than digital imagery, ILM became the leading centre for the development of <dfn class="term" data-def="Imagery produced by computer software and composited into live-action footage, used to create environments, characters or effects that cannot be filmed practically.">computer-generated imagery (CGI)</dfn> over the following decade. Two films marked the tipping points of practical possibility.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18">In <em>Terminator 2: Judgment Day</em> (1991, directed by James Cameron), the T-1000 antagonist was rendered as a liquid-metal shape-shifter: its surface rippled, reformed, and mimicked other surfaces. The sequence in which it reforms from a puddle of mercury &mdash; beginning with a flat, reflective pool on the floor, then gathering into a standing figure, its surface resolving from abstract silver to the face and uniform of a police officer &mdash; demonstrated that CGI could now create convincing organic movement in a way no physical effect could match. In <em>Jurassic Park</em> (1993, directed by Steven Spielberg), CGI dinosaurs were integrated with live-action photography in extended wide shots: a herd of herbivores stampeding across a sunlit valley, the animals casting shadows on real ground, their legs moving in the dust. The integration was seamless enough that audiences understood they were watching something that could not be filmed because it did not exist. Both films used <dfn class="term" data-def="The digital process of combining computer-generated elements with live-action footage so that they appear to exist in the same physical space.">compositing</dfn> techniques to embed generated images within photographed environments.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: the Steadicam was invented by a studio, not a person</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">A common error is to credit the Steadicam to a studio (often Panavision or ILM) rather than to its actual inventor. The Steadicam was developed by Garrett Brown, an independent inventor and camera operator. Brown patented the device and licensed it to the industry &mdash; it was not developed inside a studio&rsquo;s R&amp;D department. Knowing the inventor&rsquo;s name is important: exam questions specifically ask for Garrett Brown, not a corporate name.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The 1948 Paramount decree ended vertical integration in Hollywood, forcing studios to sell their cinema chains. Combined with the television threat, it drove a wave of technological spectacle in the 1950s &mdash; widescreen formats, 3D, colour &mdash; designed to offer audiences something their home screens could not.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The Steadicam, invented by Garrett Brown in 1975, transformed camera movement by combining the fluidity of a dolly shot with the freedom of handheld work. Its most celebrated use &mdash; Kubrick&rsquo;s <em>The Shining</em> (1980) &mdash; demonstrates how a specific camera technology can produce macro-level psychological effects.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Early CGI milestones &mdash; <em>Terminator 2</em> (1991) and <em>Jurassic Park</em> (1993) &mdash; demonstrated that computer-generated imagery could be composited seamlessly into live action, opening possibilities for storytelling that practical effects could not achieve.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">For &ldquo;identify&rdquo; or &ldquo;name&rdquo; questions on this topic, precision is everything. Students often confuse CinemaScope (1953, 20th Century Fox) with the Paramount decree (1948) &mdash; these are different developments, different years, different studios. Similarly, the Steadicam was invented by Garrett Brown in 1975 &mdash; not by a studio or by ILM. For &ldquo;explain the effect&rdquo; questions, always state the consequence for filmmakers or audiences, not just the fact of the technology. For example: &ldquo;The Steadicam allowed directors to create smooth continuous tracking shots through complex spaces without rails or dollies, enabling a new kind of psychological effect that neither pure handheld nor pure dolly work could produce.&rdquo; That final clause &mdash; what it could do that other methods could not &mdash; is what earns the effect marks.</p>

