--- DESCRIPTION ---
Toy Story, iPhone filmmaking, Netflix and IMAX: how digital technology democratised cinema.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Toy Story and the First Computer-Generated Feature: 1995</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">In 1995, Pixar Animation Studios released <em>Toy Story</em>, the world&rsquo;s first feature-length film made entirely with <dfn class="term" data-def="A film made entirely from images generated by computer software, with no live-action photography; distinct from films that merely incorporate CGI elements alongside photographed footage.">computer-generated animation</dfn>. Directed by John Lasseter and co-produced with Disney, the film ran for 77 minutes and was generated entirely by computers &mdash; no hand-drawn frames, no physical sets, no photographed actors. Every surface, shadow, and movement was calculated by software. The film was a commercial and critical success, and it announced a new era in animated filmmaking.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">A critical distinction is worth fixing in memory here: <em>Toy Story</em> was not the first film to use computer-generated imagery. Live-action films had been incorporating CGI elements since the 1980s, and significant milestones &mdash; the <em>Terminator 2</em> and <em>Jurassic Park</em> achievements of the early 1990s &mdash; preceded it. What made <em>Toy Story</em> historically significant was that it was the first <em>feature-length</em> film composed entirely of computer-generated images, with no photography involved in the image-making. This distinction matters: exam questions specifically ask about the &ldquo;first computer-generated feature&rdquo;, not the &ldquo;first film with CGI&rdquo;.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory: what was Toy Story's significance, who directed it, which studio made it, and what year was it released?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p><em>Toy Story</em> (1995), directed by John Lasseter at Pixar Animation Studios (co-produced with Disney), was the world&rsquo;s first feature-length computer-generated animated film. It was not the first film with CGI, but the first feature composed entirely of computer-generated images.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Digital Cinematography and the DV Revolution</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Alongside the rise of computer animation, the 1990s and 2000s brought a revolution in how live-action films were captured. Film stock &mdash; <dfn class="term" data-def="Light-sensitive plastic film coated with chemical emulsion, used as the recording medium for cinema from its earliest days until the digital era.">celluloid</dfn> &mdash; had been the standard recording medium since cinema&rsquo;s earliest days. It was expensive to buy, expensive to process, and limited in quantity: a director had a finite number of takes before the cost became prohibitive. <dfn class="term" data-def="A method of capturing moving images directly onto electronic storage as digital data rather than onto photographic film, removing the per-foot cost of film stock.">Digital cinematography</dfn> changed this calculation fundamentally. Digital sensors recorded to memory cards or hard drives; footage could be reviewed immediately on set; takes were essentially free. This changed the economics and culture of production.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Consumer-grade digital video (DV) cameras also began to appear in the late 1990s: small, lightweight, affordable, and capable of producing images that could be broadcast or projected. The &ldquo;DV revolution&rdquo; made it possible for filmmakers without studio budgets or studio equipment to produce feature-length work. Directors such as Lars von Trier used consumer DV cameras deliberately, as an aesthetic and political statement about cinema&rsquo;s relationship to expensive technology. The rough, video-like quality of DV footage &mdash; its specific colour response, its softness, its visible grain in low light &mdash; became a recognisable aesthetic in independent filmmaking of the late 1990s and 2000s.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Citizen Filmmaking: Tangerine and the Smartphone</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">By the 2010s, smartphone cameras had reached a quality that made serious filmmaking possible on a device that fits in a pocket. The landmark moment in mainstream cinema came in 2015 with <em>Tangerine</em>, directed by Sean Baker. The film was shot entirely on an iPhone 5S using the Filmic Pro app and anamorphic lenses attached to the phone. It premiered at the Sundance Film Festival and received wide critical acclaim, demonstrating that a compelling, professional-quality narrative feature could be made for a fraction of the cost of conventional film production.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><em>Tangerine</em>&rsquo;s visual approach was partly a consequence of its shooting method. The iPhone&rsquo;s wide-angle lens naturally produced a specific look: faces often filled much of the frame, backgrounds fell away into soft-focus colour, and the camera could move through the bustle of Los Angeles streets with an immediacy that a conventional film camera rig could not match. The film&rsquo;s energy and intimacy were inseparable from the technology used to make it. This is a key principle: technology does not just determine what is <em>possible</em>; it shapes the aesthetic character of what is made.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What &lsquo;democratisation of production&rsquo; means &mdash; and why it matters</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">The phrase &ldquo;<dfn class="term" data-def="The process by which falling costs and widening access to production technology make filmmaking available to people and communities who were previously excluded by the high cost of equipment.">democratisation of production</dfn>&rdquo; refers to the way that digital and smartphone technology reduced the cost barrier to making films. In the era of celluloid, a feature-length film required expensive film stock, laboratory processing and specialist camera equipment &mdash; barriers that effectively restricted filmmaking to those with institutional backing. Digital cameras, editing software and smartphone devices removed most of those barriers. Anyone with a phone and a free editing app can now shoot and distribute a short film. The question this raises &mdash; relevant for Film Studies at any level &mdash; is what this means for whose stories get told and which voices get heard. Films like <em>Tangerine</em> told stories about communities (transgender sex workers in Los Angeles) that the Hollywood studio system had historically ignored, using equipment that the studio system would never have sanctioned.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Netflix and the Streaming Revolution</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Netflix was founded in 1997 as a postal DVD rental service. In 2007, it launched its online <dfn class="term" data-def="A method of delivering film and television content over the internet directly to the viewer&rsquo;s device, without downloading the file and without the need for physical media such as DVD or Blu-ray.">streaming</dfn> service, initially for a small catalogue of titles. Growth was rapid. By 2013 Netflix was producing its own original content; by 2016 it had over 80 million subscribers globally. The tipping point came in 2017 when streaming overtook DVD as the primary distribution method for film and television content in the United States. Physical media &mdash; the disc in the box on the shelf &mdash; had been the dominant home-viewing format since the 1980s; within a decade of Netflix&rsquo;s streaming launch, it was secondary.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The implications for cinema were profound. Films could reach global audiences without a theatrical release; directors who might not have attracted studio backing found Netflix willing to fund adventurous projects. The traditional window between theatrical release and home viewing, which had been several months, compressed dramatically &mdash; and with the Covid-19 pandemic of 2020 closed cinemas worldwide, some major releases went directly to streaming. For audiences, streaming meant access to an enormous catalogue on demand, at home, without scheduling. For the film industry, it raised questions about what &ldquo;cinema&rdquo; means when most viewers watch films on a laptop or phone screen.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: in what year did Netflix launch its streaming service, and in what year did streaming overtake DVD as the primary home-viewing format in the US?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Netflix launched its streaming service in 2007. By 2017, streaming had overtaken DVD as the primary distribution format for film and television content in the United States, fundamentally changing how audiences accessed cinema.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">IMAX and the Blockbuster Spectacle: Avengers: Infinity War (2018)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">At the same time as smartphone cinema was making production cheaper and more accessible, the premium end of filmmaking was pushing in the opposite direction: towards the largest, highest-resolution cameras ever used in mainstream fiction production. The <dfn class="term" data-def="A large-format film and digital camera and projection system producing images significantly larger and sharper than standard 35mm film, used for a premium theatrical experience.">IMAX</dfn> format had been used in documentary and nature films since the 1970s and in partial sequences of mainstream releases from the early 2000s. In 2018, <em>Avengers: Infinity War</em>, directed by Anthony and Joe Russo, became the first major fiction feature to be shot <em>entirely</em> on IMAX cameras. Every frame was captured at the maximum resolution available, producing an image quality that could fill a six-storey screen with detail visible to the last row of the auditorium. The film was designed to be a theatrical event &mdash; an argument that cinema in a premium auditorium offers an experience categorically different from anything available at home.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>A common error: &lsquo;IMAX&rsquo; vs &lsquo;shot on IMAX cameras&rsquo;</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Many students write that a film was &ldquo;shown in IMAX&rdquo; and think this is the same as saying it was &ldquo;shot on IMAX cameras&rdquo;. These are completely different things. Most films shown in IMAX cinemas are standard productions digitally upscaled for the large screen &mdash; the IMAX branding refers to the <em>presentation</em> format, not the <em>capture</em> format. <em>Avengers: Infinity War</em> (2018) was historically significant because it was the first major fiction feature shot <em>entirely</em> using IMAX cameras during production. The difference in image quality between footage shot on IMAX cameras and footage upscaled for IMAX projection is substantial. Do not conflate the two in exam answers.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21"><em>Toy Story</em> (1995, Pixar/Disney, directed by John Lasseter) was the world&rsquo;s first feature-length computer-generated animated film &mdash; not the first film with CGI, but the first feature made entirely without photography.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Digital and smartphone technology democratised film production: <em>Tangerine</em> (2015, Sean Baker) was shot on an iPhone 5S, demonstrating that low-cost tools could produce professional, acclaimed narrative cinema and tell stories the studio system ignored.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Netflix launched streaming in 2007; by 2017 it had overtaken DVD as the primary distribution format. Meanwhile, <em>Avengers: Infinity War</em> (2018) became the first major fiction feature shot entirely on IMAX cameras &mdash; the highest and lowest cost ends of the industry developing in parallel.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">The most frequent error on this topic is confusing <em>Toy Story</em> (1995) &mdash; the first feature-length computer-generated film &mdash; with the first film to use CGI. CGI appeared in live-action films earlier in the 1990s; <em>Toy Story</em> was the first <em>entirely</em> computer-generated feature. Similarly, many students write &ldquo;IMAX&rdquo; when they mean &ldquo;shot entirely on IMAX cameras&rdquo; &mdash; these are different things, and the exam will test the distinction. For &ldquo;explain the effect&rdquo; questions, the key move is to state what the technology changed for filmmakers <em>or</em> audiences. The democratisation of production question invites you to say: cheaper digital technology made it possible for directors outside the studio system to tell stories that would otherwise not have been made. Give a specific example: <em>Tangerine</em> (2015), Sean Baker, iPhone 5S.</p>

